WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.165
Lesson 2.8.

00:00:02.165 --> 00:00:04.030
The MEV supply chain.

00:00:04.030 --> 00:00:06.702
By the end, you'll understand who sees, orders

00:00:06.702 --> 00:00:09.373
and profits from your transactions, and how to

00:00:09.373 --> 00:00:10.965
get some of that value back.

00:00:12.265 --> 00:00:13.555
Here's the plan.

00:00:13.555 --> 00:00:16.245
The full path your transaction actually takes,

00:00:16.245 --> 00:00:18.720
from your wallet to a confirmed block.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.441
Order-flow auctions and rebates, and how you can

00:00:21.441 --> 00:00:23.795
actually get some of that value back.

00:00:23.795 --> 00:00:26.340
What changes once you're on a layer two, with

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.240
a single sequencer instead of this whole chain.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:31.927
And a full worked example, showing exactly how

00:00:31.927 --> 00:00:34.100
differently the same trade can end up.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:36.310
The full path.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:40.618
Here's exactly where your transaction goes, step by

00:00:40.618 --> 00:00:42.885
step, from the moment you sign it.

00:00:42.885 --> 00:00:45.742
Your wallet sends it, either to the public

00:00:45.742 --> 00:00:48.799
mempool, visible to everyone, or to a private

00:00:48.799 --> 00:00:53.020
R.P.C., an M.E.V.-protection endpoint that keeps it hidden.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:58.173
Searchers scan constantly for opportunities, arbitrage, liquidations, backruns,

00:00:58.173 --> 00:01:02.145
sandwiches, and submit bundles competing to capture them.

00:01:02.145 --> 00:01:05.525
Builders assemble the most profitable possible block, from

00:01:05.525 --> 00:01:08.447
all the transactions and bundles available to them.

00:01:09.747 --> 00:01:11.941
And here's the fourth and final stop,

00:01:11.941 --> 00:01:14.897
before your transaction is actually confirmed.

00:01:14.897 --> 00:01:18.793
Relays pass completed blocks along to proposers, the validators

00:01:18.793 --> 00:01:21.407
who actually add a block to the chain.

00:01:21.407 --> 00:01:24.738
Those proposers simply pick whichever block pays

00:01:24.738 --> 00:01:28.001
them the most, a system called proposer-builder

00:01:28.001 --> 00:01:32.312
separation, run through infrastructure called M.E.V.-Boost.

00:01:32.312 --> 00:01:35.448
Your transaction, wherever it landed in that winning

00:01:35.448 --> 00:01:38.110
block, is now confirmed, for better or worse.

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:41.579
Here's the entire path, laid out as

00:01:41.579 --> 00:01:43.989
one picture, worth holding in your head

00:01:43.989 --> 00:01:45.795
as a single mental model.

00:01:45.795 --> 00:01:47.711
Your wallet, to the mempool or

00:01:47.711 --> 00:01:50.915
a private R.P.C. Searchers, submitting bundles.

00:01:50.915 --> 00:01:52.949
Builders, assembling blocks.

00:01:52.949 --> 00:01:55.344
Relays, passing them to proposers.

00:01:55.344 --> 00:01:57.490
Every one of those five stops is a

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:00.432
separate party, each one capable of seeing, and

00:02:00.432 --> 00:02:03.804
acting on, your pending transaction before it confirms.

00:02:05.104 --> 00:02:08.561
One real number, so this whole chain isn't abstract.

00:02:08.561 --> 00:02:13.137
Blockchain researchers have tracked hundreds of millions of dollars

00:02:13.137 --> 00:02:16.771
in total M.E.V. extracted across Ethereum in a single

00:02:16.771 --> 00:02:22.051
year, spread across arbitrage, liquidations and sandwich attacks combined.

00:02:22.051 --> 00:02:24.976
This is outside research, not this program's own

00:02:24.976 --> 00:02:27.305
data, but it's the actual scale of the

00:02:27.305 --> 00:02:29.991
supply chain this lesson just walked through.

00:02:31.292 --> 00:02:32.382
Quick check.

00:02:32.382 --> 00:02:34.547
What do builders actually do, in this chain?

00:02:38.967 --> 00:02:41.631
The answer: they assemble the most profitable

00:02:41.631 --> 00:02:44.527
possible block, out of the available transactions

00:02:44.527 --> 00:02:46.844
and searcher bundles, to earn the right

00:02:46.844 --> 00:02:48.697
to have it chosen by a proposer.

00:02:49.997 --> 00:02:51.222
Getting value back.

00:02:52.522 --> 00:02:56.381
Here's how an M.E.V. rebate actually works, mechanically,

00:02:56.381 --> 00:03:00.407
since “getting value back” can sound abstract otherwise.

00:03:00.407 --> 00:03:04.036
Some private R.P.C.s and wallets let searchers bid

00:03:04.036 --> 00:03:07.736
directly for the right to backrun your transaction,

00:03:07.736 --> 00:03:11.167
the harmless kind of M.E.V., not a sandwich.

00:03:11.167 --> 00:03:13.272
The highest bidder wins that right.

00:03:13.272 --> 00:03:15.344
And a share of what they bid gets

00:03:15.344 --> 00:03:18.392
refunded straight back to you, the sender, simply

00:03:18.392 --> 00:03:20.647
for having routed through that R.P.C.

00:03:21.947 --> 00:03:24.741
Worth connecting this directly to Lesson two

00:03:24.741 --> 00:03:27.660
point six, since intent-based trading solves a

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:30.382
related problem from a different angle.

00:03:30.382 --> 00:03:33.617
Intent systems move the entire competition to

00:03:33.617 --> 00:03:36.993
solvers, before your transaction even exists as

00:03:36.993 --> 00:03:39.806
a signed transaction at all, often with

00:03:39.806 --> 00:03:43.412
M.E.V. protection built into the system itself.

00:03:43.412 --> 00:03:48.412
A protected R.P.C. with rebates protects an existing transaction.

00:03:48.412 --> 00:03:50.362
An intent system changes how the

00:03:50.362 --> 00:03:52.312
trade happens in the first place.

00:03:53.612 --> 00:03:56.190
Here's how to actually check whether your own

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:58.881
wallet already offers this, since it's often on

00:03:58.881 --> 00:04:01.367
by default without being named clearly.

00:04:01.367 --> 00:04:05.412
Open your wallet's network or R.P.C. settings directly.

00:04:05.412 --> 00:04:08.816
Look for a name like “protected,” “private,” or

00:04:08.816 --> 00:04:13.017
a specific R.P.C. provider known for M.E.V. protection.

00:04:13.017 --> 00:04:16.325
And check its own documentation specifically for whether

00:04:16.325 --> 00:04:18.588
it pays rebates, and how large a share

00:04:18.588 --> 00:04:20.387
it actually passes back to you.

00:04:21.687 --> 00:04:24.277
Worth being honest about the actual size

00:04:24.277 --> 00:04:27.357
of a rebate, so expectations stay realistic.

00:04:27.357 --> 00:04:29.373
A rebate is a share of a backrun

00:04:29.373 --> 00:04:32.306
opportunity's value, not a share of your entire

00:04:32.306 --> 00:04:35.056
trade; it's typically a modest amount, not a

00:04:35.056 --> 00:04:37.067
meaningful yield on its own.

00:04:37.067 --> 00:04:39.360
The real value of routing correctly is

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.830
avoiding the sandwich in the first place,

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:43.717
from Lesson two point five.

00:04:43.717 --> 00:04:45.735
The rebate is a genuine bonus on

00:04:45.735 --> 00:04:47.692
top of that, not the main event.

00:04:48.992 --> 00:04:50.082
Quick check.

00:04:50.082 --> 00:04:52.247
What is an MEV rebate, specifically?

00:04:56.667 --> 00:04:58.891
The answer: a refund of part of the

00:04:58.891 --> 00:05:01.363
value searchers bid to win the right to

00:05:01.363 --> 00:05:04.267
backrun your transaction, paid back to you for

00:05:04.267 --> 00:05:07.047
routing through a private RPC that offers it.

00:05:08.347 --> 00:05:09.562
L2 sequencers.

00:05:10.862 --> 00:05:14.155
Here's the actual difference worth holding clearly, since

00:05:14.155 --> 00:05:17.237
an L2 changes almost everything from this lesson.

00:05:17.237 --> 00:05:21.112
Ethereum mainnet runs the full chain: searchers, builders,

00:05:21.112 --> 00:05:24.827
relays, proposers, each a separate, competing party.

00:05:24.827 --> 00:05:27.795
Most layer twos today instead run a single

00:05:27.795 --> 00:05:32.695
sequencer, one operator, ordering every transaction directly, commonly

00:05:32.695 --> 00:05:36.353
on a first-come-first-served or a priority-fee basis.

00:05:37.653 --> 00:05:40.843
Worth knowing where this is actually heading, since “a

00:05:40.843 --> 00:05:44.972
single sequencer” isn't necessarily the permanent state of things.

00:05:44.972 --> 00:05:48.992
Today, most L2s run one operator, ordering everything.

00:05:48.992 --> 00:05:53.394
Several roadmaps now point toward shared or decentralised sequencing,

00:05:53.394 --> 00:05:57.405
spreading that same ordering power across multiple parties.

00:05:57.405 --> 00:06:00.493
And the actual M.E.V. dynamics on any given

00:06:00.493 --> 00:06:03.370
L2 will keep shifting as that rolls out,

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:07.230
worth rechecking periodically, not assumed to be fixed.

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:11.659
Worth being precise about what this actually means

00:06:11.659 --> 00:06:14.290
for you, practically, on any given L2.

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:17.745
It's genuinely worth knowing that specific L2's own

00:06:17.745 --> 00:06:21.067
ordering rule, since it directly shapes what kind

00:06:21.067 --> 00:06:23.560
of M.E.V. is even possible there.

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:27.661
A single, trusted sequencer removes the multi-party competition

00:06:27.661 --> 00:06:30.737
this lesson just walked through, but it doesn't

00:06:30.737 --> 00:06:34.006
automatically remove every form of M.E.V.; it just

00:06:34.006 --> 00:06:36.505
changes who's positioned to capture it.

00:06:37.805 --> 00:06:38.810
Worked example.

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:44.298
Here's the same one-hundred-thousand-dollar swap, sent two different

00:06:44.298 --> 00:06:47.815
ways, with one percent slippage tolerance either way.

00:06:47.815 --> 00:06:50.980
Sent publicly, to the open mempool: up to roughly

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:54.462
one thousand dollars could leak straight to a sandwich

00:06:54.462 --> 00:06:58.180
bot, exactly the mechanism from Lesson two point five.

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:01.273
Sent through a protected R.P.C. with rebates

00:07:01.273 --> 00:07:04.023
instead: no sandwich reaches it at all,

00:07:04.023 --> 00:07:06.360
since it was never visible to see.

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:10.505
And here's the part worth naming specifically,

00:07:10.505 --> 00:07:12.988
since the protected route isn't just “no

00:07:12.988 --> 00:07:15.225
loss,” it can be a genuine gain.

00:07:15.225 --> 00:07:17.552
If that same trade happens to create a

00:07:17.552 --> 00:07:20.954
backrun opportunity, say, an arbitrage between two pools

00:07:20.954 --> 00:07:23.640
it touches, you may actually receive a share

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:26.564
of that value back, through the rebate mechanism

00:07:26.564 --> 00:07:28.535
from earlier in this lesson.

00:07:28.535 --> 00:07:29.735
Same trade.

00:07:29.735 --> 00:07:30.980
Same size.

00:07:30.980 --> 00:07:34.062
A meaningfully different financial outcome, decided

00:07:34.062 --> 00:07:35.840
entirely by where you sent it.

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:38.230
Quick check.

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:41.710
Sending the exact same $100,000 swap through a

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:45.633
protected RPC with rebates, instead of publicly, can

00:07:45.633 --> 00:07:49.335
result in what, beyond simply avoiding a sandwich?

00:07:53.755 --> 00:07:56.799
The answer: potentially receiving a share of a

00:07:56.799 --> 00:08:00.360
backrun opportunity's value back, as a rebate, turning

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:03.145
a possible loss into a possible small gain.

00:08:04.445 --> 00:08:06.859
Here's the actual decision, boiled down

00:08:06.859 --> 00:08:09.150
to when protection pays off most.

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:11.546
It matters most on large trades, where

00:08:11.546 --> 00:08:14.610
a sandwich's absolute dollar take is largest.

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:17.686
It matters most on volatile pairs, where backrun

00:08:17.686 --> 00:08:21.250
opportunities, and rebate value, show up most often.

00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:23.998
And it matters least on tiny, routine trades,

00:08:23.998 --> 00:08:26.866
where neither the risk nor the potential rebate

00:08:26.866 --> 00:08:29.375
is large enough to change much either way.

00:08:30.675 --> 00:08:32.135
Here's your checklist.

00:08:32.135 --> 00:08:34.710
Do it now, before your next large trade.

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:38.281
Large trades go through a protected R.P.C., an

00:08:38.281 --> 00:08:42.460
intent system, or an aggregator with M.E.V. protection

00:08:42.460 --> 00:08:45.115
built in, not the open mempool.

00:08:45.115 --> 00:08:47.358
You know specifically whether your

00:08:47.358 --> 00:08:49.900
own wallet's R.P.C. offers rebates.

00:08:49.900 --> 00:08:52.749
And you understand your own L2's sequencer ordering

00:08:52.749 --> 00:08:55.215
rules, since they're not the same everywhere.

00:08:56.515 --> 00:08:58.347
One last thing worth naming,

00:08:58.347 --> 00:09:00.605
closing out this entire module.

00:09:00.605 --> 00:09:04.817
Every lesson in Module Two, price impact, slippage, impermanent

00:09:04.817 --> 00:09:08.766
loss, L.V.R., sandwich defence, order types, and this exact

00:09:08.766 --> 00:09:12.517
supply chain, is really one connected skill: knowing the

00:09:12.517 --> 00:09:15.281
real cost of a trade before you commit to

00:09:15.281 --> 00:09:18.045
it, from every direction it can come from.

00:09:19.345 --> 00:09:20.590
Let's recap.

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:23.705
Your transaction passes through the mempool or

00:09:23.705 --> 00:09:27.018
a private R.P.C., searchers, builders, and relays

00:09:27.018 --> 00:09:30.035
to proposers, before it's ever confirmed.

00:09:30.035 --> 00:09:33.154
Order-flow auctions can rebate you a share of the

00:09:33.154 --> 00:09:37.570
value a backrun opportunity creates, simply for routing correctly.

00:09:37.570 --> 00:09:40.992
Most L2s replace that whole chain with a single

00:09:40.992 --> 00:09:45.925
sequencer, which changes, but doesn't eliminate, M.E.V. dynamics.

00:09:45.925 --> 00:09:48.544
And the exact same trade, sent two different

00:09:48.544 --> 00:09:52.095
ways, can produce a meaningfully different financial outcome.

00:09:53.395 --> 00:09:56.942
Route large trades through protection, know your wallet's

00:09:56.942 --> 00:10:00.300
rebate policy, and know your L2's sequencer rules.

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:02.225
That's Module 2, complete.

00:10:02.225 --> 00:10:05.080
Next up, Module 3: Lending and leverage.
